  <div class="container prose" style="max-width:76ch">
    <p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Importing from India follows a predictable sequence — confirm specification and price, agree payment terms, review samples if needed, sign a contract, then track production, inspection, documentation and shipping through to your port. The two things that trip up first-time importers most are unclear specifications and not confirming import documentation requirements early.</p>

    <h2>Step 1 — Confirm what you actually need</h2>
    <p>Before contacting any exporter, nail down: exact product and variety, quantity, target price range (research typical FOB/CIF ranges first), destination port, and any documentation your country requires for that product category (phytosanitary rules for plant products, health certificates for seafood, import permits for some categories).</p>

    <h2>Step 2 — Verify the exporter</h2>
    <p>Ask for their IEC (Import Export Code) and, for agricultural products, APEDA registration. A legitimate Indian export business will have both and should be willing to share them. See our guide question below on verifying an exporter for more detail.</p>

    <h2>Step 3 — Get a written quote and specification</h2>
    <p>A serious quote should include price, Incoterm, packaging, specification (grade/size/moisture as applicable), and a validity window. Vague quotes without a specification are a red flag.</p>

    <h2>Step 4 — Request samples if the order size justifies it</h2>
    <p>For a first order at meaningful volume, a sample is worth the time and any cost. Check the exporter's sample policy for cost and lead time.</p>

    <h2>Step 5 — Agree payment terms appropriate to the order size</h2>
    <p>For first orders, a Letter of Credit gives both sides protection. For smaller trial orders, an advance/CAD or TT split is common. See our <a href="/payment-terms/">Payment Terms</a> guide.</p>

    <h2>Step 6 — Confirm documentation requirements before shipment, not after</h2>
    <p>Different countries and product categories require different documents — phytosanitary certificates, certificates of origin, fumigation certificates, health certificates, lab test reports. Share your country's requirements with the exporter before the goods ship, not when they arrive at your port and customs asks for a document you never requested.</p>

    <h2>Step 7 — Track the shipment and prepare for import clearance</h2>
    <p>Once shipped, track the vessel and have your customs broker (or yourself, if you're handling it directly) ready with the shipping documents before arrival, to avoid demurrage charges from delayed clearance.</p>

    <h2>How do I verify an Indian exporter before I pay?</h2>
    <p>Ask for their IEC and APEDA (or relevant) registration numbers and cross-check them are willing to provide documentation. Request references from existing buyers if possible. Start with a smaller trial order before committing to a large advance payment. Be cautious of any supplier unwilling to discuss specification in detail or who pressures for full advance payment on a first, large order.</p>

    <h2>What's the biggest mistake first-time importers make?</h2>
    <p>Not confirming specification and documentation requirements in enough detail before the order ships. A vague spec ("Grade A rice") means different things to different suppliers — pin down measurable criteria (moisture %, admixture %, grain length, broken percentage, etc.) in writing.</p>
